        ,
          "choices": ["trunc", "round"]
        },
        {
          "param": "k",
          "low": 2,
          "high": 4
        ,
          "choices": null
        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.5,
      "why": "unrolled_k raises throughput (k rotations/cycle) so it can meet 50 MSPS at a lower Fmax, which can reduce the power index relative to iterative even with a wider datapath. k=2..4 keeps the combinational depth modest so Fmax stays high; larger k would risk Fmax and add area/power. Same accuracy knobs (N, guards, rounding) as iterative, so this family lets the search trade a bit more area/power for the throughput headroom and see whether it lands on a better power point of the Pareto front."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Only iterative and unrolled_k are available to me, and both are the low-power shared-datapath families (pipelined/pipelined_m are not in my set). The hard constraints are throughput_msps >= 50 and max_abs_err <= 2^-12 (0.000244141). For iterative, throughput = Fmax, so Fmax must be >= 50 MHz; with N in 12..20 the critical path is short enough that this is comfortably met, so the real trade-off is accuracy vs power. For unrolled_k, throughput = k*Fmax, so k=2..4 gives headroom to run at a lower Fmax (lower power) while still clearing 50 MSPS. Accuracy is set by N, angle_guard, frac_guard and rounding (identical bits across families), so I sweep N 12..20 and the guards in their low ranges, and keep both rounding modes to capture the ~0.5-bit accuracy/power trade. data_width 14..20 brackets the output LSB (2^-(W-2)) needed to express ~12-bit accuracy without wasting area. I split the budget 50/50 so the search maps the power-vs-accuracy front in both families and the merged front can reveal whether unrolled_k's throughput headroom buys a lower power_index at equal accuracy. Ranges are kept tight to focus the 100-eval budget on the region where the constraints are actually binding."
}

{
  "decision": "add_family",
  "rationale": "The current best throughput is 8.73 MSPS (iterative), which is only ~17% of the required 50 MSPS. The iterative and unrolled_k families are structurally limited because they produce one result every several cycles (N+3 or ceil(N/k)+3 cycles). To achieve 50 MSPS, we need a pipelined architecture that produces one result per cycle. The pipelined family has a latency of N+2 cycles but a throughput of 1 result/cycle. If we can achieve an Fmax of at least 50 MHz, the throughput will be >= 50 MSPS. Given that the iterative design at W=18 had an Fmax of ~7.27 MHz (implied by 7.27 MSPS throughput with 1 result per ~1 cycle? No, iterative is 1 result per N+3 cycles. If throughput is 7.27 MSPS, and N=15, cycles=18. Fmax = 7.27 * 18 = 130 MHz? Wait. Throughput = Fmax / latency_cycles. 7.27e6 = Fmax / 18 => Fmax = 130 MHz. This is a very high Fmax for sky130. Let's re-read the metric definition: 'throughput_msps: estimated results per microsecond = Fmax x results/cycle'. For iterative, results/cycle is 1/(N+3). So Throughput = Fmax / (N+3). If Throughput is 7.27 MSPS and N=15, then Fmax = 7.27 * 18 = 130.86 MHz. This seems high but possible for a simple CORDIC. However, the constraint is throughput >= 50 MSPS. For pipelined, results/cycle = 1. So Throughput = Fmax. We need Fmax >= 50 MHz. The iterative design achieved 130 MHz Fmax. A pipelined design with similar width (W=18) and N=15 should have a comparable or slightly lower Fmax due to pipeline registers, but likely well above 50 MHz. Therefore, the pipelined family is the only one that can meet the throughput constraint. We must explore it.",
